menu "Spatial Card"

    config CARD_ENABLE_BLE
        bool "Enable the BLE Nordic UART link (NimBLE)"
        depends on BT_NIMBLE_ENABLED
        default y
        help
            Vision Pro link. Disable (together with CONFIG_BT_ENABLED=n, see
            sdkconfig.noble) for the browser simulator or BLE-less bring-up.

    config CARD_SCREEN_WIDTH_MM
        string "Active display width in mm"
        default "36.72"
        help
            Mirrors assets/card/card.json device.activeAreaMm[0]. Reported in the
            hello message as screenWidthMm (informational).

    config CARD_DEFAULT_THEME
        string "Theme shown at boot when the screen is locked"
        default "base"
        help
            Theme id the screen is pinned to while CARD_SCREEN_LOCK is on
            ("base", "neon-city" or "data-nebula"; anything else maps to "base").
            With the lock off this value is unused and the theme comes from NVS.

    config CARD_SCREEN_LOCK
        bool "Lock the screen to CARD_DEFAULT_THEME"
        default y
        help
            The ARKit reference object was trained with CARD_DEFAULT_THEME on the
            screen, so the pixels must never change for the life of that scan.
            With the lock on the card boots into that theme whatever NVS
            remembers, idle UP/DOWN no longer cycle the theme (the button events
            are still reported on serial / BLE), and `set_theme` from either link
            is refused with ack ok:false err:"locked". `strip`, `set_mode`,
            `stats`, `celebrate` and `backlight` are unaffected, and `glow` is a
            transient overlay and unaffected (it restores the pixels underneath
            exactly, so it is not a theme change).

    config CARD_AR_BACKLIGHT_PERCENT
        int "AR-mode backlight level (percent)"
        range 10 100
        default 85
        help
            Level used while a BLE central is connected; 100 = DC. Below 100 the
            backlight runs on the 5 kHz / 10-bit LEDC PWM, which is far above the
            headset camera frame rate and therefore invisible to it, so a lower
            level is a real duty cycle that stops the card over-exposing under room
            light. The `backlight` command overrides this for the running AR
            session; `set_mode ar` re-applies this default. 85 is the V3-4 round's
            choice, NOT a measured result -- the v2 plan's experiment E1 (100/60/40)
            was never run on hardware, so treat 85 as a starting point that the
            Hub's `backlight` command retunes live. It is pinned here rather than in
            sdkconfig.defaults so the isolated tools/validate.sh --firmware build
            gets it too, and it leaves the button blink (CARD_BUTTON_BLINK) room to
            flash UP to 100 instead of dipping.

    config CARD_BUTTON_BLINK
        bool "Blink the backlight on every button press"
        default y
        help
            AR mode keeps the screen pixel-static, so a press would otherwise
            produce no feedback on the card at all. With this on, every click /
            long press in AR mode blinks the backlight *toward the far end* of
            the range for 120 ms: from the 85 % default it flashes UP to 100 and
            holds it, and only a session already at 100 goes the other way (down
            to 55 % for 60 ms, then halfway back, then the exact level). A screen
            that dims on a key press reads as a glitch; one that flashes brighter
            reads as a response. Backlight only: no repaint, no
            `will_change`/`changed`, no change to the `backlight` value reported
            in `hello` / `battery`, and a no-op in idle mode or while the
            backlight is off.

    config CARD_CUE_VOLUME
        int "Cue playback volume (0-100)"
        range 0 100
        default 70

endmenu
